---
import { sitePath } from '../lib/paths';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import PersonCard from '../components/PersonCard.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CEE_PROFILE_URL, FACULTY_PROFILE_URL, LAB_NAME } from '../lib/site';

const pi = (await getCollection('people')).find((p) => p.data.role === 'pi');
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={`About the ${LAB_NAME} at Tufts University.`}>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width: var(--measure-narrow);">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A geotechnical lab at Tufts CEE working on climate-resilient and equitable infrastructure.
        The lab combines fundamental soil mechanics with applied work alongside communities and
        infrastructure partners.
      </p>

      <h2>Challenge</h2>
      <p>
        Climate is changing the loading on infrastructure faster than much of that infrastructure
        was designed to accept. Drought, flooding, wildfire, and compound or cascading sequences
        do not stay in the atmosphere. They wet, dry, heat, and weaken the soils that hold slopes,
        dams, and levees, then cascade into the communities those systems are meant to protect.
        The people most exposed are often the least represented in the original design. Treating
        climate as a new load case while leaving equity to another chapter misses both the
        mechanics and the harm.
      </p>

      <h2>Contribution</h2>
      <p>
        The lab works at the interface of civil and geotechnical engineering with climate change,
        resilient infrastructure, and environmental justice. The focus is the resilience and
        adaptation of critical infrastructure to extreme events in a changing climate, with
        particular attention to disadvantaged communities. That is a lab problem, not a personal
        brand: show how wetting, drying, and heat change the performance of earthen systems, and
        keep who sits behind those systems inside the analysis. Support has included NSF, USDA,
        NOAA, NASA, and DOD, among others.
      </p>

      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>
        Fundamental work starts from variably saturated soils and earthen structures under
        multi-physics processes: flow, heat, and stress kept in one analysis rather than added
        later as a correction. Applied work takes the same mechanics to climate-loaded slopes,
        dams, and levees. Collaboration with climate science, hydrology, geosciences, social
        science, remote sensing, and data science is part of the method because the failure
        sequence does not stay inside one department. The lab sits in Civil and Environmental
        Engineering at Tufts.
      </p>

      <h2>Principal investigator</h2>
      <p>
        Farshid Vahedifard is Professor of Civil and Environmental Engineering at Tufts and the
        Louis Berger Chair. His research sits at the interface of geotechnical engineering,
        climate, and environmental justice, with emphasis on how extreme events affect
        infrastructure and disadvantaged communities. Full profile:{' '}
        <a href={CEE_PROFILE_URL}>Tufts CEE</a>
        {' · '}
        <a href={FACULTY_PROFILE_URL}>facultyprofiles.tufts.edu</a>.
      </p>
      {pi && <PersonCard person={pi} />}

      <p class="about-actions">
        Interested in the lab's work? Read the <a href={sitePath('/projects')}>project list</a>, review
        <a href={sitePath('/publications')}>publications</a>, or <a href={sitePath('/contact')}>contact the lab</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
